  "solution": {
   "javascript": "var isBalanced = function(root) {\n    function heightOrFailure(node) {\n        if (node === null) return 0;\n        const leftHeight = heightOrFailure(node.left);\n        if (leftHeight === -1) return -1;\n        const rightHeight = heightOrFailure(node.right);\n        if (rightHeight === -1) return -1;\n        if (Math.abs(leftHeight - rightHeight) > 1) return -1;\n        return 1 + Math.max(leftHeight, rightHeight);\n    }\n    return heightOrFailure(root) !== -1;\n};",
   "python": "from typing import Optional\n\n# TreeNode is supplied by the LeetCode judge.\nclass Solution:\n    def isBalanced(self, root: Optional[\"TreeNode\"]) -> bool:\n        def height_or_failure(node: Optional[\"TreeNode\"]) -> int:\n            if node is None:\n                return 0\n\n            left_height = height_or_failure(node.left)\n            if left_height == -1:\n                return -1\n\n            right_height = height_or_failure(node.right)\n            if right_height == -1:\n                return -1\n\n            if abs(left_height - right_height) > 1:\n                return -1\n            return 1 + max(left_height, right_height)\n\n        return height_or_failure(root) != -1"
  },
  "approach": "Compute heights bottom-up with a postorder DFS that returns a failure sentinel (-1) as soon as an unbalanced subtree appears. If either child reports the sentinel, propagate it; otherwise compare the two heights, returning the sentinel when their difference exceeds one and the actual height when it does not. Each node's height is computed once, so time is O(n). The recursion stack occupies O(h) auxiliary space for tree height h.",
